{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLhnCqgvgH2c3sKkGWE5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015XtXHgjpWrKmFyjSfUbykp","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n -i trufflehog CLAUDE.md | head; grep -n -i \"needs-human\" docs/proposal.md | grep -i label | head -5; grep -n -i -A8 \"Open issues\" .ard/217/worker-engineer.md | head -30; go vet -tags integration ./internal/roles/ 2>&1 | tail -5"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13712,"cache_read_input_tokens":11271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13712},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"81af7507-d74f-4b94-8acb-7541f7a2c620","uuid":"ccb5d1e6-60d5-40e1-bf2d-737c83291ef1","timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:10:26.479Z","request_id":"req_011ChLhnCiVdc177UUGrPFio"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015XtXHgjpWrKmFyjSfUbykp","type":"tool_result","content":"50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n183:**Route.** The scanner key `route` decides where its issues go: `pending` (default for executable scanners) files with `ard:pending`, so they enter triage; `none` (default for LLM scanners) files with the scanner label only, and a human adds `ard:pending` to send one issue to triage; `needs-human` files with `ard:needs-human` and no `ard:pending`, so no agent triages them. LLM scanners may not use `pending`. Changing `route` affects only new issues. A human promotes one issue by removing `ard:needs-human` and/or adding `ard:pending`; after that ard never joins findings onto it.\n206:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending` and are never candidates; #204 may widen this) for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n239:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.\n19:## Open issues\n20-- A maintainer needs to run `TestTrufflehogDocker` with Docker and network, and paste the PASS lines into the PR.\n21-- CLAUDE.md is unchanged: its Layout section points to `docs/layout.md`, which already lists the role, as the plan says. The label table already covers `ard:needs-human` at filing.\ngo: downloading github.com/google/uuid v1.6.0\ngo: downloading modernc.org/mathutil v1.7.1\ngo: downloading modernc.org/memory v1.12.1\ngo: downloading github.com/ncruces/go-strftime v1.1.0\ngo: downloading github.com/remyoudompheng/bigfft v0.0.0-20230129092748-24d4a6f8daec","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"81af7507-d74f-4b94-8acb-7541f7a2c620","uuid":"b33b7173-a212-4ca3-89b1-f78ce712e5a6","timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:10:40.955Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n183:**Route.** The scanner key `route` decides where its issues go: `pendi